    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`, and all 7 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 3 new ones).\n\n**How it works:**\n- It fetches documents with `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. This is the standard call every LangChain retriever supports, so it works the same for `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. The tag goes on this one call only; the retriever object itself isn't changed.\n- It goes through the documents in the order the retriever returned them and skips any whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used. It stops once it has `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `[n] <source>`, a newline, then the document text with surrounding whitespace removed. Entries are joined with a blank line. If nothing is found, it returns `\"\"`.\n\n**New tests** in `tests/test_context.py`:\n- Repeated sources are skipped, output is capped at 3 entries, and numbering and whitespace trimming are correct.\n- A search with no hits returns an empty string.\n- The retriever call carries the `kb-context` tag, checked with a callback handler that records the tags it receives.\n\nA document with no `\"source\"` in its metadata will show as `[n] None`, and any later documents without a source are treated as repeats of it and skipped. Your notes always set `source`, so this only matters if production documents might not.\n\nThe project notes say to use Context7 for library docs, but it returned \"Monthly quota exceeded\". I confirmed how tags are passed by reading the installed `langchain_core/retrievers.py` instead. No dependencies were added."
  },
